/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A busbar is a thick copper or aluminium bar.
   - A split bus (also called an isolated bus) gives each generator its own bar.
   - The gain is fault containment.
   - The price is that a generator failure strands its bus.
   - On a parallel bus all generators close onto one bar.
   - Once closed, the machines stay in step and share the load.
   - V is the terminal voltage in volts, V₀ is the no-load voltage in volts, k is the droop coefficient in volts per ampere, and I is the generator current in amperes.
   - Two generators on one bus must sit at the same terminal voltage, so for equal no-load voltages the one with the smaller k takes the larger current.
   - Split-parallel is the normal state of most twin-engine transports.
   - When one generator drops offline its GCB opens and the BTB closes, so the surviving generator feeds both buses.
*/
