/* book exam traps: Assuming parallel generators must have exactly equal no-load voltages. They share by droop, so equal set points with unequal slopes still give unequal currents. | Paralleling AC generators without a synchronism check. Closing out of phase gives a huge circulating current and can damage the drive. | Confusing the generator circuit breaker (GCB) with the bus tie breaker (BTB): the GCB joins one generator to its own bus, the BTB joins the two buses. | Treating a split bus as redundancy. It contains faults, but a lost generator leaves its DC loads on the battery or a TRU and its AC loads dead, until a tie is closed. | Leaving out the suppression diode across a DC relay coil, so the inductive kickback pits the controlling switch contacts. */
